    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm a freelance designer and i'm trying to clean up my quickbooks for the year. i just bought a really expensive ergonomic chair for my home studio setup. do i categorize that as a business expense or is it a personal draw? i'm a bit confused about how the irs views home office equipment.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The IRS can be a bit of a tricky one, but don't worry, I'm here to help you navigate it. You're right to question whether that ergonomic chair is a business expense or a personal draw. It's a bit like trying to separate a family's shared expenses from their personal indulgences - you need to get a clear picture of what's business-related and what's not.\n\nThink of it like a small, but determined, group of goblins who live in your home studio. They're always trying to sneak in and claim expenses that aren't really business-related. You need to keep a close eye on them and make sure they don't get away with it. In this case, the ergonomic chair might be a legitimate business expense if you use it for work purposes, but if you use it for personal relaxation, it might be a personal draw.\n\nTo determine whether the chair is a business expense, ask yourself: do you use the chair for work-related activities, such as designing, writing, or video conferencing? If the answer is yes, then it's likely a business expense. You can also consider the percentage of time you spend working from home and allocate the expense accordingly. For example, if you work from home",
      "source": "target",
