    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's get back to the tax stuff.\n\nThe IRS allows you to deduct the full cost of the ergonomic chair as a business expense in the year you purchase it, but only if you use it regularly and exclusively for business purposes. This is known as a \"current deduction.\" However, if you don't meet the exclusive use test, you may need to depreciate the chair over its useful life, which is typically 5-7 years for office furniture.\n\nThink of it like a small, but clever, business owner who tries to squeeze every last penny out of their expenses. They might try to deduct the full cost of the chair in the first year, but the IRS is like a wise old wizard who says, \"No, no, no! You must follow the rules and depreciate it over time.\" In this case, you'll need to keep track of the chair's depreciation and claim it as a business expense over several years.\n\nAs for the depreciation method, you can use the Modified Accelerated Cost Recovery System (MACRS) or the Section 179 deduction. The MACRS method allows you to depreciate the chair over a set period of years, while the Section",
